\f1\fs22 Everything ached, seemingly from her bone marrow out. Sarah felt like someone had swapped out her bespoke skeletal system for a rental. None quite fitted. The last renter probably hadn\'92t even had it dry-cleaned. They\'92d spilt cocktails on it in a crowded bar; they\'92d eaten prawn rendang and smoked cheap coronas; they took it out dancing and possibly fell under a garbage truck on the way home. No, it was a train. Perhaps they\'92d fallen off a cliff while abseiling.\
She threw up into a pillow slip, and wondered if Eloise had domestic help locked in the bunker, on the off chance she\'92d need household staff one day. She placed the pillow, vomit up, on the floor of the built-in and swore she\'92d get back to it.\
\'93Whatever, you\'92re trying to say, Little Clinton say it,\'94 Sarah pleaded. \'93For tax sake.\'94 \
Her retinas were still reporting fractals like veils obscuring the orthogonal rigour of the bedroom geometry. Worse, the images were howling like harpies come to carry her away.\
Sarah decided she\'92d rather carry herself away, thank you very much. And why were they all women? She\'92d thank her hallucinations to be less sexist.\
Little Clinton kicked. The harpies stopped howling. And grew penises. Sarah was not convinced that was an improvement. The fractals swirled and faded, and Sarah had received a feeling, of sitting in a particular chair, and the chair was at Sarah\'92s station in the Ironing Room.\
Sarah needed a shower; maybe she\'92d feel better after she\'92d cooked her skin and steamed her bones.\
When she\'92d run enough hot water to make town capacity planners reassess local energy consumption forecasts, she cut the water, stepped out and patted herself dry.\
Kathy was waiting for her, sitting on her bed. Sarah\'92s loosely wrapped towel fell to the floor. Kathy fetched it swiftly and helped Sarah get wrapped again.\
\'93Going somewhere?\'94 Kathy asked. \'93I think all the nightclubs for fifty clicks would be shut by now.\'94\
\'93Something is going on in the Ironing Room,\'94 Sarah answered then winced hard and sat on the edge of the bed.\
\'93Contractions?\'94\
\'93Irregular. Braxton hicks,\'94 Sarah lied.\
\'93Lie back.\'94 Kathy placed her hand on Sarah\'92s shoulder. \'93Let me examine you.\'94\
Sarah resisted. \'93Help me get dressed.\'94\
\'93Please lie down.\'94\
Sarah resisted. \'93I\'92ll be fine.\'94\
\'93Sarah, I think you understand this is different.\'94\
\'93Is it?\'94 Sarah sat more upright. \'93I\'92m a survivor.\'94\
Kathy tilted her head. \'93I want you to survive this birth.\'94\
\'93I will. You\'92re here.\'94\
\'93I\'92ll come with you.\'94\
Sarah put her hand on Kathy\'92s forearm. \'93I\'92m fine.\'94\
Kathy pulled back. \'93Yes, I know. You\'92re 
\f2\i invincible
\f1\i0 .\'94 Her tone made it clear the notion was ill-informed. \'93Feeling invincible is not the same as being invincible.\'94\
\'93A mother always knows,\'94 Sarah said.\
\'93So does a doctor,\'94 Kathy said with enough emphasis for Sarah to look at here sideways. \'93A doctor\'85 knows. Especially a doctor who has decoded a couple of viruses and became curious about the bloodwork of her most important patient.\'94\
Sarah pulled back as if shoved. \'93You tested my blood for the viruses without telling me.\'94\
Kathy shrugged. \'93You carried the Chilli virus all this time without telling me.\'94\
Sarah answered with silence.\
\'93Which of you is patient zero: you or Chanta?\'94\
Sarah\'92s head fell.\
\'93You carried the cure to Pickle all this time and you told no one?\'94\
The features of Sarah\'92s face wrenched in all directions. \'93I didn\'92t\'85 know\'85 it would\'85\'94\
\'93I think you did.\'94\
\'93Do you know how long I\'92ve had to carry this, understanding the consequences anyone I slept with becoming\'85\'94\
\'93Invincible? But they don\'92t, Sarah. The efficacy degrades.\'94\
\'93I didn\'92t know that!\'94\
Kathy chose her words. \'93How did Chanta\'97I mean, not that it matters to me personally\'85\'94\
Sarah laughed like a harpy. \'93I was so careful! Truly, I\'92ve been 
\f2\i so
\f1\i0  careful. Since I was a teenager\'85 can you imagine?\'94\
Kathy wiped a tear away. \'93No. I cannot imagine.\'94\
They hugged.\
For a long time.\
\'93I have to go,\'94 Sarah whispered.\
Kathy nodded over Sarah\'92s cradled head.\
They separated.\
\'93Go be with Hayley,\'94 Sarah instructed.\
After Sarah left, Kathy sat on the edge of the bed while moments stretched. In one of them, Kathy tore her patches off and let her misery move.
